    $u406c64a8${"version":3,"generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:16+00:00","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","tour":{"overview":"This repository is Sentry's JavaScript SDK family, a set of packages that capture errors, traces, replays and feedback from browsers, servers and edge runtimes. The Core client, scope and event pipeline is the shared base, and the Span and tracing model sits inside it. The OpenTelemetry bridge and the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation feed tracing into the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, with AI/LLM client instrumentation layered on top. The Browser SDK and browser-utils, Session Replay and Feedback, the Framework SDK packages, the Next.js SDK and the Cloudflare SDK adapt that base to specific platforms. Bundler plugins and sourcemap upload handle build-time concerns.","sections":[{"unit_id":"otel-bridge","paragraph":"This unit makes Sentry's scopes, spans, sampling and trace propagation run on top of OpenTelemetry. It sits beneath the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, which use it, and it moves in step with them and with the Span and tracing model and the Core client, scope and event pipeline. The decision is that Node-family SDKs hand span and context management to OpenTelemetry through a custom propagator, sampler and async-context strategy. Sentry's own Node tracing was not kept, so the SDK can reuse the large ecosystem of OpenTelemetry instrumentations. On history, Lukas Stracke, working alone, switched SvelteKit to the OpenTelemetry-based Node package in March 2024 [human]. That is the only commit recorded, and it does not explain the bridge's design in detail."},{"unit_id":"orchestrion","paragraph":"This unit instruments third-party libraries by transforming their code at build or load time, so that they publish tracing channels. It is used by the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation and by AI/LLM client instrumentation, and it changes together with those and with the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to use syntax-tree transforms, delivered through bundler plugins, module loader hooks and a separate runtime injection package, with per-library configuration. Runtime monkey-patching through require-in-the-middle or OpenTelemetry instrumentation was not chosen, because the transforms work with ES modules and bundlers. Francesco Gringl-Novy changed the exports alone in August 2026 [human]. In September 2026 Gringl-Novy split out the injection package with Claude Code [assisted]."},{"unit_id":"core-sdk","paragraph":"This is the platform-agnostic foundation: the client, scopes, integrations, and the steps that prepare an event, wrap it in an envelope and send it through a transport. It uses the Span and tracing model, and that model and AI/LLM client instrumentation use it back. It changes together with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Span and tracing model. The decision is a single shared package with a model that has no central hub object, and platform SDKs only wrap it. Per-platform cores and a hub-based design were not chosen, so browser, server and edge behave the same. Abhijeet Prasad introduced the transports API in 2022 [human]. Francesco Gringl-Novy folded the utilities into core in 2025 [human]."},{"unit_id":"tracing-core","paragraph":"This unit creates and manages spans and traces inside core, covering sampling, idle spans, the dynamic sampling context, span streaming and trace propagation. It uses the Core client, scope and event pipeline and is used by it, and AI/LLM client instrumentation also uses it. It changes with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the core pipeline and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to implement spans natively, with an async-context strategy that OpenTelemetry can override, plus a newer streamed-span mode. OpenTelemetry-only tracing and a transaction-only model were not used, because the browser needs lightweight tracing while servers get OpenTelemetry through the override. Recent history is test ports to span streaming in late 2026 by Martin Sonnberger and Nicolas Hrubec with OpenAI Codex, and Sonnberger's Svelte end-to-end port with Claude Code [assisted]."},{"unit_id":"ai-instrumentation","paragraph":"This unit produces spans for AI libraries such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Vercel AI, LangChain, Mastra and MCP. It uses the Span and tracing model, the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation and the Core client, scope and event pipeline. The Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation uses it. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, Orchestrion and the Cloudflare SDK. The decision is a separate wrapper or subscriber per library, each emitting spans that follow the gen-ai semantic conventions. Generic HTTP-only tracing was not chosen, since it would miss model, token and tool details. Beyond the fast-growing AI surface, the code does not show why. The history available for these paths lists no commits, so it does not show who shaped it or when."},{"unit_id":"node-sdk","paragraph":"This unit covers Node initialization, HTTP and fetch instrumentation, process-level integrations, and a runtime-agnostic server-utils package whose integrations Bun, Deno, Cloudflare and others reuse. It uses the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation, AI/LLM client instrumentation and the OpenTelemetry bridge. It changes with the Framework SDK packages, Orchestrion and the AI instrumentation. The decision is to move database, AI and framework instrumentations out of the Node package into server-utils, using diagnostics-channel subscribers. Keeping a separate OpenTelemetry instrumentation per library inside the Node package was the alternative, and it would have tied that work to Node alone. Francesco Gringl-Novy, working alone, consolidated the server-utils integration exports in August 2026 [human]. The history does not otherwise explain the move."},{"unit_id":"browser-sdk","paragraph":"This is the browser SDK. It provides the browser client, global error handlers, breadcrumbs, browser tracing, many CDN bundle variants, and a companion browser-utils package for web vitals and performance instrumentation. It builds on core and the tracing model, and it changes with the Core client, scope and event pipeline, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is an integration-based SDK with many pre-composed bundle entry points, with performance code kept in a separate package. A single monolithic bundle and lazy-loading everything were not chosen, because the aim is bundle-size control and tree-shaking. Martin Sonnberger, working alone, made the latest change in July 2026, extracting performance mark and measure spans [human]."},{"unit_id":"framework-sdks","paragraph":"These are thin packages for React, Vue, Svelte and SvelteKit, Angular, Nuxt, Remix, Astro, Nest, Hono and others. Each adapts Sentry to one framework's router and error handling. They sit on top of the browser, node or cloudflare SDKs and change together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Browser SDK and browser-utils, and the Next.js SDK. The decision is one small layer per framework, each with its own router and error hooks. One universal SDK was not chosen, because each framework has its own lifecycle and routing. Lukas Stracke, working alone, switched SvelteKit to the OpenTelemetry-based Node package in March 2024 [human]. That is the only commit recorded, and it says little about the other framework packages."},{"unit_id":"nextjs","paragraph":"This is the SDK for Next.js, with client, server and edge entry points, build configuration wrapping for webpack and turbopack, route wrapping and use-cache instrumentation. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Framework SDK packages and the Browser SDK and browser-utils. The decision is a build-time configuration wrapper with loaders that wrap routes and components, plus separate client, server and edge runtimes. Runtime-only instrumentation was not chosen, because Next.js runs code in several runtimes and build-time wrapping reaches code that runtime patching cannot. The only history is a 2025 fix by Charly Gomez, handling async route parameters when extracting URLs, written alone [human]. The history does not show why the overall design was chosen."},{"unit_id":"cloudflare","paragraph":"This is the SDK for Cloudflare Workers. It instruments handlers, Durable Objects, Workflows, queues and bindings, and propagates trace context. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Framework SDK packages and the Browser SDK and browser-utils. The decision is to wrap handlers and bindings explicitly and carry trace context through RPC, fetch and queue messages. A Node-compatibility SDK without wrapping was not chosen, because of the Workers runtime's constraints. Jan Peer Stöcklmair added RPC propagation alone in April 2026 [human]. Later changes to RPC and queue propagation were made with Claude Code by Stöcklmair and Arthur Fiorette [assisted], in August and November 2026."},{"unit_id":"bundler-plugins","paragraph":"This unit provides the plugin logic that injects debug IDs and uploads source maps for rollup, vite, webpack, esbuild and rolldown. Thin per-bundler wrapper packages sit on top of it. It changes together with the Framework SDK packages, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Next.js SDK. The decision is one core plugin manager with per-bundler adapters. Separate implementations for each bundler were not chosen. The code was consolidated from an external plugin repository and is tested with many fixtures. The history available for these paths lists no commits, so it does not show who shaped it or when, and it does not explain the consolidation beyond what the code layout suggests."},{"unit_id":"replay-feedback","paragraph":"This unit records user sessions with rrweb, compresses the data in a web worker, and provides the feedback widget and screenshots. It changes together with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the Core client, scope and event pipeline, and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to keep replay in an internal package, compress with fflate in the worker, and build the feedback interface on preact. pako for compression and a React interface were not used, for size and performance. Francesco Novy switched to fflate in November 2023 [human]. Sigrid Huemer moved the replay code into the internal package in March 2024 [human]. Both worked alone, and the history does not explain the choice of preact."}],"basis":"483d2282478b","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:51:15+00:00"},"units":[{"id":"core-sdk","name":"Core client, scope and event pipeline","kind":"module","summary":"Platform-agnostic Client, Scope, integrations, envelope building and transports that every SDK package builds on.","decision":"A single shared core package holding the hub-less scope/client model, event preparation, envelope and transport base; platform SDKs only wrap it.","alternatives":"Per-platform duplicated cores; a hub-based model.","why":"Shared behaviour across browser, server, edge and frameworks; utils were folded into core (utils-hoist removal).","paths":["packages/core/src/client.ts","packages/core/src/scope.ts","packages/core/src/exports.ts","packages/core/src/index.ts","packages/core/src/integration.ts","packages/core/src/envelope.ts","packages/core/src/transports","packages/core/src/utils/prepareEvent.ts","packages/core/src/server-runtime-client.ts"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C1","C1.2","C1.3"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"76acf2f","date":"2022-03-21","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Abhijeet Prasad","subject":"feat(core): Introduce New Transports API (#4716)"},{"sha":"ceb003c","date":"2025-06-18","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Francesco Gringl-Novy","subject":"ref(core): Remove `utils-hoist` folder (#16639)"}],"importance":0.651,"orientation":{"purpose":"The shared foundation every platform SDK builds on. It holds the client, scopes, integrations, and the steps that turn an event into an envelope and send it through a transport.","decision":"One core package holds a hub-less scope/client model, event preparation, and the envelope and transport base. Platform SDKs only wrap it. Per-platform cores and a hub-based model were not chosen, to share behaviour across browser, server and edge. Utilities were later folded into core when utils-hoist was removed.","where":[{"path":"packages/core/src/client.ts","what":"base Client class"},{"path":"packages/core/src/scope.ts","what":"scope data model"},{"path":"packages/core/src/utils/prepareEvent.ts","what":"event preparation pipeline"}],"shaped_by":"Humans alone: Abhijeet Prasad introduced the transports API in 2022, and Francesco Gringl-Novy removed utils-hoist in 2025 [human].","basis":"3eaebae0b228","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:47+00:00"}},{"id":"tracing-core","name":"Span and tracing model","kind":"module","summary":"Span creation, sampling, idle spans, dynamic sampling context, span streaming and propagation in core.","decision":"Spans implemented natively in core (SentrySpan, trace.ts) with an async-context strategy that OpenTelemetry can override, plus a newer streamed-span mode.","alternatives":"OpenTelemetry-only tracing; transaction-only model.","why":"Browser needs lightweight tracing; server uses OTel via the strategy hook; streaming migration visible in recent test ports.","paths":["packages/core/src/tracing","packages/core/src/asyncContext","packages/core/src/utils/spanUtils.ts","packages/core/src/fetch.ts","packages/core/src/utils/tracePropagationTargets.ts"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C1.1","C1.7"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"40e0275","date":"2026-10-09","klass":"assisted","agent":"ope