ly tracing.","why":"Rapidly growing AI feature surface; unclear beyond that.","paths":["packages/server-utils/src/ai","packages/core/src/integrations/mcp-server","packages/server-utils/src/integrations/vercel-ai"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.379,"history":[],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Creates spans for AI and LLM libraries such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Vercel AI, LangChain, Mastra and MCP.","decision":"Each library gets its own wrapper or subscriber that emits gen-ai semantic-convention spans. Generic HTTP-only tracing was not used. Beyond the growing AI surface, the code and history don't show why.","where":[{"path":"packages/server-utils/src/ai","what":"shared AI helpers and types"},{"path":"packages/server-utils/src/integrations/vercel-ai","what":"Vercel AI integration"},{"path":"packages/core/src/integrations/mcp-server","what":"MCP server instrumentation"}],"shaped_by":"The history list does not say.","basis":"cc43d2d103b3","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:47+00:00"}},{"id":"framework-sdks","name":"Framework SDK packages","kind":"module","summary":"React, Vue, Svelte(Kit), Angular, Nuxt, Remix, React Router, Astro, Solid(Start), TanStack Start, Nest, Hono, Elysia, Effect layers.","decision":"Thin framework packages layered on browser/node/cloudflare SDKs with framework-specific router and error hooks.","alternatives":"One universal SDK.","why":"Each framework has distinct lifecycle/routing.","paths":["packages/react","packages/vue","packages/sveltekit","packages/angular","packages/nuxt","packages/remix","packages/react-router","packages/astro","packages/nestjs","packages/hono","packages/tanstackstart-react","packages/solidstart"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.76,"history":[{"sha":"b0e1db0","date":"2024-03-15","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Lukas Stracke","subject":"feat(sveltekit): Switch to Otel-based `@sentry/node` package (#11075)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Thin packages that adapt Sentry to each framework: React, Vue, Svelte(Kit), Angular, Nuxt, Remix, Astro, Nest and others. They add router and error hooks.","decision":"Each framework package layers on the browser, node or cloudflare SDKs with framework-specific router and error hooks. One universal SDK was not chosen, because each framework has its own lifecycle and routing.","where":[{"path":"packages/react","what":"React error boundary and router integrations"},{"path":"packages/sveltekit","what":"SvelteKit client and server"},{"path":"packages/nestjs","what":"Nest server integration"}],"shaped_by":"Lukas Stracke, a human working alone, switched SvelteKit to the OTel-based @sentry/node in March 2024 [human].","basis":"e73d435c61ac","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:47+00:00"}},{"id":"replay-feedback","name":"Session Replay and Feedback","kind":"module","summary":"rrweb-based recording with worker compression, plus feedback widget and screenshots.","decision":"Replay in replay-internal with fflate compression in a web worker; feedback UI built on preact.","alternatives":"pako compression; React UI.","why":"Size and performance (fflate switch).","paths":["packages/replay-internal/src","packages/replay-worker/src","packages/replay-canvas/src","packages/feedback/src"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.399,"history":[{"sha":"10fb09a","date":"2023-11-03","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Francesco Novy","subject":"ref(replay): Use fflate instead of pako for compression (#9436)"},{"sha":"1c10b30","date":"2024-03-22","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Sigrid Huemer","subject":"ref(replay): Move `@sentry/replay` code to `@sentry-internal/replay` (#11200)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Records user sessions with rrweb and compresses them in a worker. It also provides the feedback widget and screenshots.","decision":"Replay lives in replay-internal, with fflate compression in a web worker. The feedback UI is built on preact. pako and a React UI were not used. The reason given is size and performance.","where":[{"path":"packages/replay-internal/src","what":"replay recording logic"},{"path":"packages/replay-worker/src","what":"compression worker"},{"path":"packages/feedback/src","what":"feedback widget"}],"shaped_by":"Humans alone: Francesco Novy switched to fflate in November 2023, and Sigrid Huemer moved the replay code to replay-internal in March 2024 [human].","basis":"f7537a0fdf47","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:47+00:00"}},{"id":"bundler-plugins","name":"Bundler plugins and sourcemap upload","kind":"module","summary":"Unified rollup/vite/webpack/esbuild/rolldown plugin core for debug IDs and source map upload, with thin wrapper packages.","decision":"One core plugin manager with per-bundler adapters.","alternatives":"Separate implementations per bundler.","why":"Consolidated from external plugin repo; tested via many fixtures.","paths":["packages/bundler-plugins","packages/webpack-plugin","packages/vite-plugin","packages/rollup-plugin","packages/esbuild-plugin","dev-packages/bundler-plugin-integration-tests"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.4,"history":[],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Provides the plugin logic for debug IDs and source map upload across rollup, vite, webpack, esbuild and rolldown, with thin per-bundler wrapper packages.","decision":"One core plugin manager with per-bundler adapters, instead of separate implementations. It was consolidated from an external plugin repo and is tested with many fixtures.","where":[{"path":"packages/bundler-plugins","what":"shared plugin core"},{"path":"packages/vite-plugin","what":"example thin wrapper"},{"path":"dev-packages/bundler-plugin-integration-tests","what":"fixture-based tests"}],"shaped_by":"The history list does not say.","basis":"701d75f4ae29","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:50:47+00:00"}}],"connections":[["ai-instrumentation","core-sdk","uses",1.0],["ai-instrumentation","orchestrion","uses",2.0],["ai-instrumentation","tracing-core","uses",8.0],["core-sdk","tracing-core","uses",16.0],["node-sdk","ai-instrumentation","uses",39.5],["node-sdk","orchestrion","uses",99.5],["node-sdk","otel-bridge","uses",6.0],["tracing-core","core-sdk","uses",22.0],["ai-instrumentation","browser-sdk","together",0.59],["ai-instrumentation","bundler-plugins","together",1.1],["ai-instrumentation","cloudflare","together",3.55],["ai-instrumentation","core-sdk","together",1.0],["ai-instrumentation","framework-sdks","together",2.45],["ai-instrumentation","nextjs","together",0.67],["ai-instrumentation","node-sdk","together",53.63],["ai-instrumentation","orchestrion","together",5.46],["ai-instrumentation","tracing-core","together",1.37],["browser-sdk","bundler-plugins","together",0.73],["browser-sdk","cloudflare","together",13.78],["browser-sdk","core-sdk","together",67.35],["browser-sdk","framework-sdks","together",37.65],["browser-sdk","nextjs","together",14.88],["browser-sdk","node-sdk","together",50.83],["browser-sdk","orchestrion","together",3.15],["browser-sdk","otel-bridge","together",6.07],["browser-sdk","replay-feedback","together",17.37],["browser-sdk","tracing-core","together",35.35],["bundler-plugins","cloudflare","together",0.16],["bundler-plugins","core-sdk","together",1.14],["bundler-plugins","framework-sdks","together",3.51],["bundler-plugins","nextjs","together",1.88],["bundler-plugins","node-sdk","together",2.31],["bundler-plugins","otel-bridge","together",1.06],["bundler-plugins","tracing-core","together",0.11],["cloudflare","core-sdk","together",11.23],["cloudflare","framework-sdks","together",15.18],["cloudflare","nextjs","together",2.52],["cloudflare","node-sdk","together",29.49],["cloudflare","orchestrion","together",3.27],["cloudflare","otel-bridge","together",2.71],["cloudflare","replay-feedback","together",0.31],["cloudflare","tracing-core","together",1.94],["core-sdk","framework-sdks","together",18.57],["core-sdk","nextjs","together",5.62],["core-sdk","node-sdk","together",45.22],["core-sdk","otel-bridge","together",12.32],["core-sdk","replay-feedback","together",7.94],["core-sdk","tracing-core","together",34.39],["framework-sdks","nextjs","together",22.2],["framework-sdks","node-sdk","together",81.29],["framework-sdks","orchestrion","together",10.66],["framework-sdks","otel-bridge","together",11.11],["framework-sdks","replay-feedback","together",2.62],["framework-sdks","tracing-core","together",21.98],["nextjs","node-sdk","together",24.39],["nextjs","orchestrion","together",3.42],["nextjs","otel-bridge","together",5.06],["nextjs","replay-feedback","together",0.47],["nextjs","tracing-core","together",4.41],["node-sdk","orchestrion","together",80.44],["node-sdk","otel-bridge","together",49.13],["node-sdk","replay-feedback","together",0.58],["node-sdk","tracing-core","together",13.05],["orchestrion","otel-bridge","together",1.26],["orchestrion","tracing-core","together",0.76],["otel-bridge","replay-feedback","together",0.14],["otel-bridge","tracing-core","together",19.83],["replay-feedback","tracing-core","together",2.42]],"hubs":[],"order":["otel-bridge","orchestrion","core-sdk","tracing-core","ai-instrumentation","node-sdk","browser-sdk","framework-sdks","nextjs","cloudflare","bundler-plugins","replay-feedback"],"tour":{"markdown":"# Architecture tour: sentry-javascript\n\nWritten by `udebt tour` at `6bee2f2c` on 2026-11-15; 12 units, about 9 minutes to read. Units come in the order to learn them: each after the units it uses.\n\nThis repository is Sentry's JavaScript SDK family, a set of packages that capture errors, traces, replays and feedback from browsers, servers and edge runtimes. The Core client, scope and event pipeline is the shared base, and the Span and tracing model sits inside it. The OpenTelemetry bridge and the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation feed tracing into the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, with AI/LLM client instrumentation layered on top. The Browser SDK and browser-utils, Session Replay and Feedback, the Framework SDK packages, the Next.js SDK and the Cloudflare SDK adapt that base to specific platforms. Bundler plugins and sourcemap upload handle build-time concerns.\n\n## The map\n\n*Uses*: its code imports the other unit's (strongest first). *Used by*: how many units import it. *Changes with*: the units commits most often touch with it.\n\n1. **OpenTelemetry bridge** (`otel-bridge`): used by 1; changes with `node-sdk`, `tracing-core`\n2. **Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation** (`orchestrion`): used by 2; changes with `node-sdk`, `framework-sdks`\n3. **Core client, scope and event pipeline** (`core-sdk`): uses `tracing-core`; used by 2; changes with `browser-sdk`, `node-sdk`\n4. **Span and tracing model** (`tracing-core`): uses `core-sdk`; used by 2; changes with `browser-sdk`, `core-sdk`\n5. **AI/LLM client instrumentation** (`ai-instrumentation`): uses `tracing-core`, `orchestrion`, `core-sdk`; used by 1; changes with `node-sdk`, `orchestrion`\n6. **Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation** (`node-sdk`): uses `orchestrion`, `ai-instrumentation`, `otel-bridge`; changes with `framework-sdks`, `orchestrion`\n7. **Browser SDK and browser-utils** (`browser-sdk`): changes with `core-sdk`, `node-sdk`\n8. **Framework SDK packages** (`framework-sdks`): changes with `node-sdk`, `browser-sdk`\n9. **Next.js SDK** (`nextjs`): changes with `node-sdk`, `framework-sdks`\n10. **Cloudflare SDK** (`cloudflare`): changes with `node-sdk`, `framework-sdks`\n11. **Bundler plugins and sourcemap upload** (`bundler-plugins`): changes with `framework-sdks`, `node-sdk`\n12. **Session Replay and Feedback** (`replay-feedback`): changes with `browser-sdk`, `core-sdk`\n\n## 1. OpenTelemetry bridge (`otel-bridge`)\n\nThis unit makes Sentry's scopes, spans, sampling and trace propagation run on top of OpenTelemetry. It sits beneath the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, which use it, and it moves in step with them and with the Span and tracing model and the Core client, scope and event pipeline. The decision is that Node-family SDKs hand span and context management to OpenTelemetry through a custom propagator, sampler and async-context strategy. Sentry's own Node tracing was not kept, so the SDK can reuse the large ecosystem of OpenTelemetry instrumentations. On history, Lukas Stracke, working alone, switched SvelteKit to the OpenTelemetry-based Node package in March 2024 [human]. That is the only commit recorded, and it does not explain the bridge's design in detail.\n\nOpen first: `packages/opentelemetry/src`, `packages/node/src/sdk`.\n\n## 2. Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation (`orchestrion`)\n\nThis unit instruments third-party libraries by transforming their code at build or load time, so that they publish tracing channels. It is used by the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation and by AI/LLM client instrumentation, and it changes together with those and with the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to use syntax-tree transforms, delivered through bundler plugins, module loader hooks and a separate runtime injection package, with per-library configuration. Runtime monkey-patching through require-in-the-middle or OpenTelemetry instrumentation was not chosen, because the transforms work with ES modules and bundlers. Francesco Gringl-Novy changed the exports alone in August 2026 [human]. In September 2026 Gringl-Novy split out the injection package with Claude Code [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion`, `packages/server-runtime-injection`, `packages/server-utils/src/tracing-channel.ts`.\n\n## 3. Core client, scope and event pipeline (`core-sdk`)\n\nThis is the platform-agnostic foundation: the client, scopes, integrations, and the steps that prepare an event, wrap it in an envelope and send it through a transport. It uses the Span and tracing model, and that model and AI/LLM client instrumentation use it back. It changes together with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Span and tracing model. The decision is a single shared package with a model that has no central hub object, and platform SDKs only wrap it. Per-platform cores and a hub-based design were not chosen, so browser, server and edge behave the same. Abhijeet Prasad introduced the transports API in 2022 [human]. Francesco Gringl-Novy folded the utilities into core in 2025 [human].\n\nOpen first: `packages/core/src/client.ts`, `packages/core/src/scope.ts`, `packages/core/src/utils/prepareEvent.ts`.\n\n## 4. Span and tracing model (`tracing-core`)\n\nThis unit creates and manages spans and traces inside core, covering sampling, idle spans, the dynamic sampling context, span streaming and trace propagation. It uses the Core client, scope and event pipeline and is used by it, and AI/LLM client instrumentation also uses it. It changes with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the core pipeline and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to implement spans natively, with an async-context strategy that OpenTelemetry can override, plus a newer streamed-span mode. OpenTelemetry-only tracing and a transaction-only model were not used, because the browser needs lightweight tracing while servers get OpenTelemetry through the override. Recent history is test ports to span streaming in late 2026 by Martin Sonnberger and Nicolas Hrubec with OpenAI Codex, and Sonnberger's Svelte end-to-end port with Claude Code [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `packages/core/src/tracing`, `packages/core/src/asyncContext`, `packages/core/src/utils/spanUtils.ts`.\n\n## 5. AI/LLM client instrumentation (`ai-instrumentation`)\n\nThis unit produces spans for AI libraries such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Vercel AI, LangChain, Mastra and MCP. It uses the Span and tracing model, the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation and the Core client, scope and event pipeline. The Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation uses it. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, Orchestrion and the Cloudflare SDK. The decision is a separate wrapper or subscriber per library, each emitting spans that follow the gen-ai semantic conventions. Generic HTTP-only tracing was not chosen, since it would miss model, token and tool details. Beyond the fast-growing AI surface, the code does not show why. The history available for these paths lists no commits, so it does not show who shaped it or when.\n\nOpen first: `packages/server-utils/src/ai`, `packages/server-utils/src/integrations/vercel-ai`, `packages/core/src/integrations/mcp-server`.\n\n## 6. Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation (`node-sdk`)\n\nThis unit covers Node initialization, HTTP and fetch instrumentation, process-level integrations, and a runtime-agnostic server-utils package whose integrations Bun, Deno, Cloudflare and others reuse. It uses the Orchestrion code-transform instrumentation, AI/LLM client instrumentation and the OpenTelemetry bridge. It changes with the Framework SDK packages, Orchestrion and the AI instrumentation. The decision is to move database, AI and framework instrumentations out of the Node package into server-utils, using diagnostics-channel subscribers. Keeping a separate OpenTelemetry instrumentation per library inside the Node package was the alternative, and it would have tied that work to Node alone. Francesco Gringl-Novy, working alone, consolidated the server-utils integration exports in August 2026 [human]. The history does not otherwise explain the move.\n\nOpen first: `packages/node/src`, `packages/server-utils/src`, `packages/bun/src`.\n\n## 7. Browser SDK and browser-utils (`browser-sdk`)\n\nThis is the browser SDK. It provides the browser client, global error handlers, breadcrumbs, browser tracing, many CDN bundle variants, and a companion browser-utils package for web vitals and performance instrumentation. It builds on core and the tracing model, and it changes with the Core client, scope and event pipeline, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is an integration-based SDK with many pre-composed bundle entry points, with performance code kept in a separate package. A single monolithic bundle and lazy-loading everything were not chosen, because the aim is bundle-size control and tree-shaking. Martin Sonnberger, working alone, made the latest change in July 2026, extracting performance mark and measure spans [human].\n\nOpen first: `packages/browser/src/sdk.ts`, `packages/browser/src/index.bundle.tracing.replay.ts`, `packages/browser-utils/src`.\n\n## 8. Framework SDK packages (`framework-sdks`)\n\nThese are thin packages for React, Vue, Svelte and SvelteKit, Angular, Nuxt, Remix, Astro, Nest, Hono and others. Each adapts Sentry to one framework's router and error handling. They sit on top of the browser, node or cloudflare SDKs and change together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Browser SDK and browser-utils, and the Next.js SDK. The decision is one small layer per framework, each with its own router and error hooks. One universal SDK was not chosen, because each framework has its own lifecycle and routing. Lukas Stracke, working alone, switched SvelteKit to the OpenTelemetry-based Node package in March 2024 [human]. That is the only commit recorded, and it says little about the other framework packages.\n\nOpen first: `packages/react`, `packages/sveltekit`, `packages/nestjs`.\n\n## 9. Next.js SDK (`nextjs`)\n\nThis is the SDK for Next.js, with client, server and edge entry points, build configuration wrapping for webpack and turbopack, route wrapping and use-cache instrumentation. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Framework SDK packages and the Browser SDK and browser-utils. The decision is a build-time configuration wrapper with loaders that wrap routes and components, plus separate client, server and edge runtimes. Runtime-only instrumentation was not chosen, because Next.js runs code in several runtimes and build-time wrapping reaches code that runtime patching cannot. The only history is a 2025 fix by Charly Gomez, handling async route parameters when extracting URLs, written alone [human]. The history does not show why the overall design was chosen.\n\nOpen first: `packages/nextjs/src`, `packages/nextjs/src/config`, `packages/nextjs/src/server`.\n\n## 10. Cloudflare SDK (`cloudflare`)\n\nThis is the SDK for Cloudflare Workers. It instruments handlers, Durable Objects, Workflows, queues and bindings, and propagates trace context. It changes together with the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, the Framework SDK packages and the Browser SDK and browser-utils. The decision is to wrap handlers and bindings explicitly and carry trace context through RPC, fetch and queue messages. A Node-compatibility SDK without wrapping was not chosen, because of the Workers runtime's constraints. Jan Peer Stöcklmair added RPC propagation alone in April 2026 [human]. Later changes to RPC and queue propagation were made with Claude Code by Stöcklmair and Arthur Fiorette [assisted], in August and November 2026.\n\nOpen first: `packages/cloudflare/src/withSentry.ts`, `packages/cloudflare/src/durableobject.ts`, `packages/cloudflare/src/workflows.ts`.\n\n## 11. Bundler plugins and sourcemap upload (`bundler-plugins`)\n\nThis unit provides the plugin logic that injects debug IDs and uploads source maps for rollup, vite, webpack, esbuild and rolldown. Thin per-bundler wrapper packages sit on top of it. It changes together with the Framework SDK packages, the Node SDK and server-utils instrumentation, and the Next.js SDK. The decision is one core plugin manager with per-bundler adapters. Separate implementations for each bundler were not chosen. The code was consolidated from an external plugin repository and is tested with many fixtures. The history available for these paths lists no commits, so it does not show who shaped it or when, and it does not explain the consolidation beyond what the code layout suggests.\n\nOpen first: `packages/bundler-plugins`, `packages/vite-plugin`, `dev-packages/bundler-plugin-integration-tests`.\n\n## 12. Session Replay and Feedback (`replay-feedback`)\n\nThis unit records user sessions with rrweb, compresses the data in a web worker, and provides the feedback widget and screenshots. It changes together with the Browser SDK and browser-utils, the Core client, scope and event pipeline, and the Framework SDK packages. The decision is to keep replay in an internal package, compress with fflate in the worker, and build the feedback interface on preact. pako for compression and a React interface were not used, for size and performance. Francesco Novy switched to fflate in November 2023 [human]. Sigrid Huemer moved the replay code into the internal package in March 2024 [human]. Both worked alone, and the history does not explain the choice of preact.\n\nOpen first: `packages/replay-internal/src`, `packages/replay-worker/src`, `packages/feedback/src`.\n\n---\n\nNext: `udebt path` for the order to learn these in and the time each takes, `udebt dig <unit>` for a walkthrough of one with its code.\n","minutes":9},"walkthroughs":{"core-sdk":{"flow":"A user calls captureException on a scope with a configured client, and the resulting error event is prepared, filtered and sent as an envelope through the transport.","steps":[{"file":"packages/core/src/scope.ts","start_line":674,"end_line":693,"narration":"The scope is the entry point. It makes an event id and a synthetic Error to keep a stack trace. It then delegates to the client it holds and passes itself as the scope. With no hub, the scope holds its client directly, so a scope with no client silently drops the call and only returns an id."},{"file":"packages/core/src/client.ts","start_line":252,"end_line":274,"narration":"The base Client first rejects an object it has already captured. It then builds the event from the exception inside the client's internal task tracker, so flush can wait for it. The call returns the event id at once, and the real work continues asynchronously. This code is shared by browser, node and edge SDKs, which supply their own event-building and transport."},{"file":"packages/core/src/client.ts","start_line":1469,"end_line":1503,"narration":"The processing step checks the sample rate before any expensive work. Errors that are sampled out are rejected up front and never prepared. Otherwise it calls the shared event preparation. Event-level policy therefore lives in core and not in each platform SDK."},{"file":"packages/core/src/utils/prepareEvent.ts","start_line":74,"end_line":109,"narration":"Preparation merges the isolation scope and the current scope into one data set. It applies that data and the active span to the event, then orders the event processors, with client processors first and scope processors last. Scope data is only read at capture time, which is what makes scopes work as a hub-less model."},{"file":"packages/core/src/client.ts","start_line":1527,"end_line":1548,"narration":"After preparation, the beforeSend result is checked. A null or invalid result records a dropped-event outcome and throws a do-not-send error. That error is logged and swallowed by the capture wrapper, so the user's code never sees it."},{"file":"packages/core/src/client.ts","start_line":522,"end_line":544,"narration":"sendEvent wraps the final event in an envelope. It adds attachments, then hands the envelope to sendEnvelope. The returned promise is deliberately not awaited, and afterSendEvent fires when it resolves. sendEnvelope (around line 1248) calls the transport and catches any failure, so a send error cannot reach the caller."}],"trap":"A reader may think captureException sends the event synchronously, or that it throws when sampling, beforeSend or the transport rejects the event. In fact it returns an id immediately, and drops and send failures are swallowed or only logged in debug builds.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"tracing-core":{"flow":"A caller invokes startSpan with a name and a callback on a root (no active parent) while tracing is enabled, and the span is created, sampled, made active, run, and ended.","steps":[{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/trace.ts","start_line":54,"end_line":95,"narration":"startSpan parses the options, forks a scope, resolves the parent span, and picks either a placeholder span (when `onlyIfParent` finds no parent) or a real child/root span via createChildOrRootSpan. It then runs the callback with the span active and ends the span in the finally callback. Scope handling goes through the async-context layer, so an OTel strategy can replace how 'active' is stored."},{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/trace.ts","start_line":331,"end_line":384,"narration":"Before any real span exists, the function emits `prepareSpanScope`. The Node SDK uses it to turn a remote OTel parent into a propagation context. The function then returns non-recording spans in two cases: tracing is disabled, or a streamed span is ignored by configuration. Those placeholders still carry the trace id and captured scopes, so the DSC and propagation keep working with no sampling decision stored on the span."},{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/trace.ts","start_line":386,"end_line":436,"narration":"This is the branching point of the tracing model. A parent without `forceTransaction` gives a child span. A forced transaction under a parent gives a new root that continues the parent's trace and freezes the parent's DSC onto itself. No parent gives a root built from the scope's propagation context (incoming trace id, parent id, sampled flag, `sample_rand`), with the DSC frozen so it stays consistent downstream. The decision to continue a trace is made here, before the sampler runs."},{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/trace.ts","start_line":480,"end_line":547,"narration":"_startRootSpan makes the sampling decision. It fires the `beforeSampling` hook, which can override the parent's decision. It then calls sampleSpan unless tracing is suppressed, builds a SentrySpan carrying the `sampled` flag, and records dropped-event outcomes. Under streaming it records only a 'span' outcome, not a 'transaction' one. It then captures the scopes and emits `spanStart`."},{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/sampling.ts","start_line":15,"end_line":75,"narration":"sampleSpan is the root-span sampler. It prefers `tracesSampler` and falls back to the parent decision or `tracesSampleRate`. It rejects invalid rates and compares the trace's `sample_rand` against the rate. Because `sample_rand` is propagated, every service in a trace reaches the same decision."},{"file":"packages/core/src/tracing/sentrySpan.ts","start_line":377,"end_line":424,"narration":"When the callback finishes, activeSpan.end() reaches _onSpanEnded. It emits `spanEnd`, and standalone spans are sent on their own. Child spans are not sent on their own. A root span in streaming mode emits `afterSegmentSpanEnd` and returns, so the streaming pipeline handles it. Otherwise the root is converted to a transaction, either directly or through a deferred strategy. This is where the legacy transaction model and the newer streamed-span model split."}],"trap":"A reader may assume a span that is not sampled, or created while tracing is off, is a real span that is discarded later. In fact the code returns a lightweight non-recording placeholder, and DSC and sampling state are read from the captured scope, not from the span.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"browser-sdk":{"flow":"A page loads the browser SDK, calls init(), and later an uncaught error is thrown, which is captured as an event by the global error handler.","steps":[{"file":"packages/browser/src/sdk.ts","start_line":30,"end_line":49,"narration":"Default integrations are a plain function returning a list, not a hard-wired client feature. Error capture (globalHandlers, browserApiErrors, linkedErrors), breadcrumbs and dedupe are included. Tracing and replay are absent, so tree-shaking and size-limit can drop them."},{"file":"packages/browser/src/sdk.ts","start_line":97,"end_line":131,"narration":"init() resolves the integration list (defaults plus user-supplied), builds client options with the fetch transport and stack parser, and hands them to initAndBind with BrowserClient. Everything after this is driven by integrations. Users can replace the whole default set via defaultIntegrations."},{"file":"packages/core/src/integration.ts","start_line":109,"end_line":125,"narration":"While binding the client, core calls setupIntegration for each integration. It skips an integration whose name is already installed. setupOnce runs once per page, while setup(client) runs for each client. This is where integrations hook into the browser."},{"file":"packages/browser/src/integrations/globalhandlers.ts","start_line":26,"end_line":51,"narration":"The GlobalHandlers integration raises the stack trace limit in setupOnce. In setup(client) it registers the onerror and onunhandledrejection handlers for that client, unless the options turn them off."},{"file":"packages/browser/src/integrations/globalhandlers.ts","start_line":53,"end_line":79,"narration":"When window.onerror fires, the callback ignores the error if the active client is not the one that registered it, or if it is an error the SDK's own wrapping has already handled. Otherwise it builds an event from the error, marks it level error, and calls captureEvent with a mechanism of handled:false. From there the client's normal pipeline and the fetch transport take over."},{"file":"packages/integration-shims/src/ElementTiming.ts","start_line":1,"end_line":17,"narration":"Bundle variants such as index.bundle.tracing.replay export shims for features they leave out, such as element timing. A shim keeps the same name and API but only logs a warning. A config that works with a full bundle still runs on a smaller CDN bundle, and the bundle stays small."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that browser tracing and replay are part of the browser client itself, or that every CDN bundle has the same feature set. In fact the client only runs the integrations it is given, and tracing and replay are separate integrations. CDN entry points either export the real integration or a shim that only warns.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"node-sdk":{"flow":"A Bun app calls Sentry.init() from @sentry/bun with tracing enabled, and later loads Express and handles a request through it.","steps":[{"file":"packages/bun/src/sdk.ts","start_line":38,"end_line":44,"narration":"Bun's SDK does not define its own database, AI or framework instrumentation. When spans are enabled it just asks server-utils for the shared tracing list. This is the reuse the decision aimed at (consolidated by Francesco Gringl-Novy in Aug 2026). Without spans, none of these tracing integrations are installed."},{"file":"packages/bun/src/sdk.ts","start_line":46,"end_line":77,"narration":"The default integration list mixes Node-package integrations (http, console, process handlers) with the shared server-utils error integrations spread in at line 72. The framework integrations such as Express are in the always-on set because they also capture errors. Bun-specific integrations come last."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/integrations/index.ts","start_line":43,"end_line":97,"narration":"These two functions are the shared catalogue. The tracing-only list covers databases, queues and AI providers, with LangChain first so it can disable the provider integrations. The error list holds the web frameworks, which are registered whether or not tracing is on. Every runtime SDK picks from these lists."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/integrations/express/index.ts","start_line":13,"end_line":23,"narration":"Each integration's setup does not patch Express. It hands a subscription callback, which listens on diagnostics channels, to the orchestrion helper, along with the module names it cares about. The span-creating code is in the instrumentation file. The channels are published by code that a bundler plugin or runtime hook injects into Express."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/instrumentation.ts","start_line":104,"end_line":144,"narration":"This is where the runtime difference lives. On Bun and Deno the callback runs immediately. On Node and elsewhere it subscribes right away only if the module was already injected. Otherwise it waits for the client's module-injected event, so unused modules do not use up Node's channel budget."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/instrumentation.ts","start_line":75,"end_line":94,"narration":"The run step wraps the callback in a one-shot guard and, by default, in the wait for the async-context binding. It marks the callback as instrumented only when the subscription actually happens. That way a repeated integration setup or a second client does not subscribe twice."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that adding these integrations to the default list means Express, databases and AI calls will be traced. In fact they only subscribe to channels, and spans appear only if the app was built with the Sentry plugin or runtime hook that injects the channel publishers.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"otel-bridge":{"flow":"A Node app that calls Sentry.init with OpenTelemetry setup enabled (as sveltekit and nextjs do) receives an HTTP request carrying sentry-trace and baggage headers, and an OTel-instrumented handler starts a span in it.","steps":[{"file":"packages/node/src/sdk/index.ts","start_line":198,"end_line":242,"narration":"After the client is created and initialised, init calls the OTel setup only when `enableOpenTelemetrySetup` is true. Further down, that option defaults to false for plain Node. Only wrappers such as sveltekit and nextjs opt in, which is where Lukas Stracke's March 2024 switch to the OTel-based Node SDK shows up. Everything after this step happens only on the opted-in path."},{"file":"packages/node/src/sdk/client.ts","start_line":109,"end_line":120,"narration":"The client's `init` picks the async-context strategy before `super.init()`, so integrations see it when they set up. With the flag on, `setOpenTelemetryContextAsyncContextStrategy` makes Sentry's scope lookups read the OTel context. With it off, a plain AsyncLocalStorage strategy is installed instead. This is the point where Sentry hands scope storage over to OTel."},{"file":"packages/opentelemetry/src/asyncContextStrategy.ts","start_line":30,"end_line":78,"narration":"This code builds Sentry's scope API on top of `api.context`. `getScopes` reads the scopes from the active OTel context. `withScope` and `withSetScope` do not fork anything themselves. They put marker keys on the context and rely on the context manager to do the fork. If you change a key here, the context manager has to change with it."},{"file":"packages/opentelemetry/src/asyncLocalStorageContextManager.ts","start_line":58,"end_line":75,"narration":"This is the OTel `ContextManager` that the strategy registers globally. `with` calls `buildContextWithSentryScopes` to turn the marker keys into real forked scopes, then runs the callback inside AsyncLocalStorage. Every OTel instrumentation that calls `context.with` therefore also forks Sentry scopes."},{"file":"packages/node/src/sdk/initOtel.ts","start_line":67,"end_line":91,"narration":"`setupOtel` registers `SentryTracerProvider` as the global tracer provider and `SentryPropagator` as the global propagator. The provider is the Sentry replacement for an OTel SDK. If another provider is already registered, setup logs a warning and returns undefined. Sentry tracing is then not active, though the context strategy has already been installed."},{"file":"packages/opentelemetry/src/propagator.ts","start_line":64,"end_line":117,"narration":"For the incoming request, OTel calls `extract`. It reads the sentry-trace and baggage headers and builds a remote parent span context. It then makes sure Sentry scopes are on the context, forking a fresh scope and trace id when there is no incoming trace. A span that OTel instrumentation then starts, through `SentryTracer`, becomes a Sentry span in the continued trace."}],"trap":"A reader may assume every Node-family SDK runs on OpenTelemetry and that a custom OTel sampler makes the sampling decisions; in fact plain Node defaults the OTel setup off, and sampling is done by Sentry core when `SentryTracer` starts a span.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"orchestrion":{"flow":"A Node app using `pg` calls Sentry.init() and then loads `pg` and runs `client.query(...)`, so Sentry records a database span without monkey-patching pg.","steps":[{"file":"packages/server-runtime-injection/src/register.ts","start_line":188,"end_line":225,"narration":"Sentry.init() synchronously calls this registration, which sets a global marker so it runs only once. It also installs a diagnostics hook that is called after each module transform. On success the hook records the module name and emits `orchestrion.module-injected` to the client. This file is the package that Francesco Gringl-Novy split out with Claude Code in September 2026."},{"file":"packages/server-runtime-injection/src/register.ts","start_line":230,"end_line":269,"narration":"On a Node version with stable `Module.registerHooks`, it registers resolve and load hooks that carry the per-library config. It also patches `_compile` for CJS. A hash check keeps a file from being transformed twice when the load hook has already handled it. The rewrite happens at load time, which is why ESM works. The older `Module.register` branch just below it covers earlier Node versions."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/config/pg.ts","start_line":5,"end_line":22,"narration":"This is the per-library config the transform reads. It names the module, the version range, the file inside the package and the function to match (`Client.query`). The transformer wraps that function so it publishes on the `orchestrion:pg:query` tracing channel. Adding or changing a library means editing config like this, not writing a patch."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/instrumentation.ts","start_line":104,"end_line":144,"narration":"The pg integration does not subscribe at init. If the module is already injected (bundler, or hook ran earlier) it subscribes now. Otherwise it waits for `orchestrion.module-injected`, which fires when the hook transforms the module. This delay exists because Node caps channels in use at 1024. Bun and Deno subscribe eagerly instead."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/tracing-channel.ts","start_line":243,"end_line":256,"narration":"When the injected code runs the traced `query`, the channel's `start` event runs this store binding. It builds a span and activates it in the async-local-storage store, so child spans parent correctly. If the span getter returns nothing, the caller's context stays as it was."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/tracing-channel.ts","start_line":166,"end_line":197,"narration":"The `end`, `asyncEnd` and `error` events close the loop. Sync completion ends the span in `end`. Promise or callback completion ends it in `asyncEnd`. A failure marks the span as errored. The caller's own result is not changed."}],"trap":"A reader may assume Sentry wraps `pg` at runtime the way require-in-the-middle patches do, but the library's source is rewritten at load or build time to publish tracing channels, and the integration only subscribes to those channels.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"cloudflare":{"flow":"A Worker calls env.MY_QUEUE.send({...}) in one invocation, and a later queue() consumer invocation processes that message.","steps":[{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/instrumentations/worker/instrumentEnv.ts","start_line":43,"end_line":76,"narration":"The handler wrapper hands user code a Proxy of `env`, so binding instrumentation happens lazily on first property access. Duck typing identifies a queue and wraps it, and the per-binding opt-in (`queueTracePropagationBindings`) is resolved here and passed down as a boolean. This is the explicit-wrapping decision applied to bindings, and the opt-in keeps message mutation off by default."},{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/utils/bindingPropagation.ts","start_line":9,"end_line":19,"narration":"This is the allow-list gate. An empty or missing list means no binding propagates. String entries must match the binding name exactly, and regexes allow patterns. The RPC option (`rpcTracePropagationBindings`) uses the same resolver, and Stöcklmair's 2026 change moved RPC to this model from a global boolean."},{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/instrumentations/worker/instrumentQueueProducer.ts","start_line":97,"end_line":111,"narration":"`send` opens a `queue.publish` span. With propagation on, the trace header is read inside that span, so the carrier points at the publish span and not at its parent. The message is then copied with the trace added, and the body size is recorded afterwards because it is only known after injection."},{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/utils/queueTraceMeta.ts","start_line":5,"end_line":30,"narration":"Cloudflare Queues has no message headers, so the trace rides inside the body as a reserved key. Only plain objects can carry it. Strings, binary data and class instances pass through unchanged, so those messages get no link. The body is copied rather than mutated, and only `sentry-trace` is stored, to save space under the 128 KB limit."},{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/utils/queueTraceMeta.ts","start_line":32,"end_line":63,"narration":"On the consumer side the key is read and then removed, so user code never sees it, even when the value is invalid. A missing or malformed value gives `undefined`, and no link is made. Frozen bodies keep the key because `Reflect.deleteProperty` returns false instead of throwing."},{"file":"packages/cloudflare/src/instrumentations/worker/instrumentQueue.ts","start_line":67,"end_line":112,"narration":"The consumer starts `process <queue>` as a root span and attaches span links to each producer. It does not continue any one producer's trace, because a batch can mix unrelated traces. It captures unhandled errors and flushes with `waitUntil`."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the consumer becomes a child of the producer's trace, as with fetch or RPC. It does not: `queue.process` is always a new root span that only links to producers, and that happens only for plain-object bodies on bindings that are allow-listed.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"nextjs":{"flow":"A developer runs a Next.js build with webpack, then a browser sends a GET to an App Router route handler (app/api/foo/route.ts) that throws an error.","steps":[{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/config/withSentryConfig/index.ts","start_line":18,"end_line":42,"narration":"The user's next.config exports the result of this function. It accepts an object, function or promise-returning config and hands each to the final-config builder, so all Sentry build behaviour is attached before Next.js starts. This is the build-time half of the decision: nothing is instrumented unless the config is wrapped."},{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/config/webpack.ts","start_line":287,"end_line":315,"narration":"For server builds, the webpack config gets rules that route matching files through the Sentry loader. Rules are added to the front of the array, so they run after other transforms like TypeScript. Route handlers get their own rule, tagged with a target kind, and the whole block can be turned off by an option."},{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/config/loaders/wrappingLoader.ts","start_line":126,"end_line":167,"narration":"At build time, the loader turns the file path into a parameterized route and skips excluded routes and client components. It then picks the route-handler template. The route is baked in as a string, which is why runtime code needs no route discovery."},{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/config/loaders/wrappingLoader.ts","start_line":229,"end_line":246,"narration":"The template is bundled with the user's file by Rollup, which splits the star re-export into named exports that Next.js requires. If bundling fails, the loader warns and returns the user's code unchanged, so a wrapping failure never breaks the build."},{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/config/templates/routeHandlerWrapperTemplate.ts","start_line":27,"end_line":58,"narration":"This is the code that ends up in the user's bundle. It returns the handler untouched during the production build phase, because reading request data would make the route dynamic. At request time it wraps the handler in a proxy that reads the request headers from Next's internal async storage, with a try/catch because that API is not public. It then calls the runtime wrapper with the HTTP method and baked-in route."},{"file":"packages/nextjs/src/common/wrapRouteHandlerWithSentry.ts","start_line":84,"end_line":123,"narration":"The runtime wrapper runs the handler inside a scope and handles errors. Redirect, not-found, auth-interrupt and prerender-abort errors are control flow and only set a span status. Any other error is set as an internal error on the spans and captured as an unhandled error. Afterwards a flush is scheduled so events are sent before a serverless function freezes."}],"trap":"A reader may think the Sentry SDK instruments Next.js routes at runtime when the Sentry init code runs, but the wrapping is done at build time by webpack loaders that rewrite each route file, and the runtime wrapper only runs because of that rewrite.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"ai-instrumentation":{"flow":"A developer's app calls `openai.chat.completions.create({ model, messages, stream: true })` on a client that was wrapped with `instrumentOpenAiClient`, and we follow it until the stream is consumed and the span ends.","steps":[{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/openai/index.ts","start_line":275,"end_line":281,"narration":"This is the entry point. The user hands over their own OpenAI client and gets back a proxy. Recording options are resolved first, so the explicit option wins, then the client's genAI data-collection settings, then the default of true. Nothing is patched globally. Each library gets its own explicit wrapper like this one, which is the decision the unit records."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/openai/index.ts","start_line":227,"end_line":272,"narration":"The proxy walks the client lazily, building a dotted path such as `chat.completions.create` as the app reaches into nested objects. If the path is in the method registry, the function is replaced with an instrumented one. Other functions are only bound to the original object, because OpenAI v5 uses private fields. Nested objects get their own proxy. The cache is keyed on the original value, so replacing a method on the client invalidates the wrapper."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/openai/constants.ts","start_line":8,"end_line":17,"narration":"This registry decides which calls get spans and which gen-ai operation each maps to. Adding a new OpenAI endpoint means adding a line here, and nothing else is needed for it to be traced. The operation value becomes the span op and name, for example `gen_ai.chat`. The `decide` entry also changes how the inputs are recorded."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/openai/index.ts","start_line":147,"end_line":201,"narration":"This is the per-call wrapper. It builds the request attributes (provider, operation, model, origin `auto.ai.openai`) and sets the span name and op from them. For `stream: true` it uses `startSpanManual`, because the span has to outlive the call. It calls the real method inside the span, records the inputs if enabled, and wraps the awaited result in `instrumentStream`. If the request fails before any stream exists, the span is ended with an error here. The result is returned through `wrapPromiseWithMethods` so SDK extras like `.withResponse()` still work."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/openai/streaming.ts","start_line":256,"end_line":292,"narration":"The stream is wrapped in an async generator that passes each event through unchanged to the app. As events go by, it accumulates tokens, finish reasons, text and tool calls into a state object. The `finally` block ends the span through `endStreamSpan`, which writes the response attributes. The span therefore closes when the consumer finishes, breaks out of the loop or errors, and not when `create()` returns. Mid-stream errors set an error status and are rethrown to the app."},{"file":"packages/server-utils/src/ai/core/utils.ts","start_line":352,"end_line":384,"narration":"OpenAI and Anthropic return an APIPromise, a Promise subclass with extra methods. This proxy sends `.then`, `.catch` and `.finally` to the instrumented promise, so spans are tied to what the user awaits. Every other property comes from the original object. `withResponse` gets special handling so its `data` is also the instrumented result. This is shared code that several per-library wrappers use."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that instrumenting `create()` automatically produces spans for any OpenAI usage. In fact only methods listed in the registry, reached through the client the user wrapped, get a span. A stream span also ends only when the stream is consumed to completion, errors or is closed, not when the call returns.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"framework-sdks":{"flow":"A browser requests a page route on a SvelteKit server whose Sentry SDK was initialised with the SvelteKit `init`. The request goes through `sentryHandle`, and the page's load code then throws a 500 error.","steps":[{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server/sdk.ts","start_line":12,"end_line":31,"narration":"The SvelteKit server SDK does not reimplement anything. It takes the Node SDK's default integrations, swaps out the stock Http one, and adds SvelteKit-specific ones (frame rewriting, SvelteKit span enrichment). It also turns on OpenTelemetry setup by default because Kit emits its own OTel spans, then tags the SDK metadata as `sveltekit` on top of `node` and hands off to the Node `init`. This is the layering decision in miniature, and it is the one Lukas Stracke made when he moved SvelteKit onto the OTel-based `@sentry/node` in March 2024."},{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handle.ts","start_line":315,"end_line":339,"narration":"This is the per-request entry point that the user puts in `hooks.server.ts`. A request for the Sentry tunnel is answered right here, without ever reaching the app. A request is also treated differently if it is a SvelteKit sub-request (an internal same-origin `fetch` from a load function) or if isolation was explicitly skipped, as `initCloudflareSentryHandle` does. In that case it skips new isolation and trace continuation, so the nested `http.server` span becomes a child of the active span instead of a new root."},{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handle.ts","start_line":340,"end_line":358,"narration":"A top-level request gets its own isolation scope, so tags and user data from concurrent requests do not leak into each other. The request data is stored on that scope. Trace continuation then depends on the Kit version: if Kit's native tracing is enabled (2.31+), Kit has already continued the trace. Otherwise the SDK calls `continueTrace` using the incoming `sentry-trace` and `baggage` headers. This is framework-specific lifecycle knowledge that a universal SDK could not hold."},{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handle.ts","start_line":262,"end_line":286,"narration":"When Kit's tracing is off, the SDK starts the `http.server` span itself. It gives the span a low-cardinality name (the route id) when span streaming is on. The `catch` block reports any error thrown out of `resolve` through `sendErrorToSentry`, then rethrows so SvelteKit's own error handling still runs. The `finally` block calls `flushIfServerless`, so serverless runtimes send the event before the process is frozen."},{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/utils.ts","start_line":73,"end_line":98,"narration":"The thrown 500 is captured here. The error is first objectified so a primitive can carry the 'already seen' marker. Redirects and 4xx HttpErrors are then filtered out, because SvelteKit uses throw for control flow (`redirect()`, `error(404)`). Anything else goes to `captureException` with an `auto.function.sveltekit.<handler>` mechanism and `handled: false`."},{"file":"packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handleError.ts","start_line":69,"end_line":93,"narration":"There is a second, separate capture path. Kit calls the user's `handleError` hook for server errors, and `handleErrorWithSentry` wraps it. It skips errors that are expected (4xx or Not Found). For the rest it calls `captureException` with the `handle_error` mechanism and flushes, using Cloudflare's `waitUntil` if the context has one. Only after that does it run the user's own handler, or a default that mimics Kit's logging."}],"trap":"A reader may assume each framework package is a standalone SDK with its own transport and capture logic. In fact the SvelteKit server package calls the Node SDK's `init`, so scopes, tracing and flushing all come from the underlying SDK. The framework layer only adds routing, request wrapping and error hooks, and the error hooks reach it through two separate paths, `sentryHandle` and `handleErrorWithSentry`.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"replay-feedback":{"flow":"A recorded rrweb DOM event on a page with Replay enabled is added to the event buffer, compressed in the web worker, and later flushed as a compressed payload.","steps":[{"file":"packages/replay-internal/src/util/addEvent.ts","start_line":119,"end_line":142,"narration":"Every rrweb event first passes the gate in shouldAddEvent. It drops events when replay is paused or disabled, events older than the idle timeout, and events past maxReplayDuration. Nothing reaches compression until it passes here."},{"file":"packages/replay-internal/src/util/addEvent.ts","start_line":51,"end_line":82,"narration":"_addEvent hands the event to whatever buffer replay currently owns, after the beforeAddRecordingEvent hook (custom events only). On a checkout in buffer mode it clears the buffer first. The call is `eventBuffer.addEvent`, so this code does not know whether compression is used."},{"file":"packages/replay-internal/src/eventBuffer/EventBufferProxy.ts","start_line":14,"end_line":26,"narration":"The proxy starts on a plain in-memory array and only switches to the worker once it reports ready. That way events recorded while the worker loads are not lost, and the worker is never required."},{"file":"packages/replay-internal/src/eventBuffer/EventBufferProxy.ts","start_line":97,"end_line":141,"narration":"If the worker fails to load, the proxy logs and stays on the uncompressed fallback. If it loads, the already-buffered events are replayed into the worker and `_used` is swapped immediately, so later events land after them. WorkerDestroyedError is treated as normal teardown."},{"file":"packages/replay-internal/src/eventBuffer/EventBufferCompressionWorker.ts","start_line":63,"end_line":77,"narration":"On the main thread the event is stringified and its length is added to a running size total. Past the maximum it rejects with EventBufferSizeExceededError, which is the overflow path handled in addEvent. Otherwise the string is posted to the worker. The main thread never compresses."},{"file":"packages/replay-worker/src/Compressor.ts","start_line":31,"end_line":79,"narration":"Inside the worker, the fflate streaming Zlib compressor is fed each event as a comma-prefixed JSON fragment, so the output is one growing JSON array. finish() pushes `]`, merges the chunks and re-initialises. Francesco Novy picked fflate over pako in Nov 2023 for size."}],"trap":"A reader may assume replay events are always compressed in the worker. The proxy starts on an uncompressed array buffer and stays there if the worker fails to load, so compression is best-effort.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"},"bundler-plugins":{"flow":"A user runs `vite build` with `sentryVitePlugin()` configured, and the plugin injects debug IDs and uploads the source maps once the bundle is written.","steps":[{"file":"packages/vite-plugin/src/index.ts","start_line":1,"end_line":1,"narration":"The published `@sentry/vite-plugin` package is only a re-export of the `vite` entry in the shared core package. This is the 'thin wrapper' half of the decision: none of the logic is here, so a fix in the core reaches every wrapper."},{"file":"packages/bundler-plugins/src/vite/index.ts","start_line":25,"end_line":35,"narration":"The Vite adapter reuses the Rollup internals and passes `'vite'` as the build tool name, along with the detected Vite major version. It returns two plugin objects: the main one runs with `enforce: 'pre'`, and an optional `post` one runs with `enforce: 'post'`. The per-bundler adapter is small and only supplies Vite-specific ordering and metadata."},{"file":"packages/bundler-plugins/src/rollup/index.ts","start_line":93,"end_line":119,"narration":"The shared Rollup/Vite plugin builder creates the single build plugin manager here. It is given a logger prefix, the build tool name and the version. If the `disable` option is set, it returns a no-op plugin before any hooks are built. Every bundler adapter goes through this same manager."},{"file":"packages/bundler-plugins/src/rollup/index.ts","start_line":268,"end_line":308,"narration":"After the bundle is written, `writeBundle` takes the staged source maps, creates the release, and globs the output JS and map files (or uses the `file` output or the bundle keys). It then hands everything to `upload`. If anything throws before the hand-off, the staged maps are kept. The `finally` block always frees the artifact dependency and deletes the artifacts, so this is where the build ends up deleting the source maps."},{"file":"packages/bundler-plugins/src/core/build-plugin-manager.ts","start_line":668,"end_line":697,"narration":"The manager's `uploadSourcemaps` first checks whether upload is allowed at all. Next it opens a `debug-id-sourcemap-upload` span and registers a dependency on the build artifacts. That dependency is needed because `writeBundle` can run several times in one build (for example with the legacy plugin), and deletion must wait for every upload. The bundler-independent upload logic lives here and is shared by all adapters."},{"file":"packages/bundler-plugins/src/core/build-plugin-manager.ts","start_line":1050,"end_line":1070,"narration":"This is the gate that decides whether anything is uploaded. A missing auth token, org or project, an empty assets list, the disabled flag, or dev mode each block the upload. In every case the build carries on and the plugin only logs, so a misconfigured plugin does not fail the build."}],"trap":"A reader may assume each wrapper package such as the Vite plugin holds its own upload logic. In fact the Vite plugin is built on the Rollup internals, and all bundlers share one plugin manager that does the debug ID and upload work.","head":"6bee2f2ccccdac123199d1b610c70a3bad5c8194"}}}$u3762de45$::jsonb,
    null);
